		{
			"id": "1eh0pOWI",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 6196.008142557702,
			"y": -2892.271488298144,
			"width": 2375.999755859375,
			"height": 1485,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "#eebefa",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 4,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "aUV",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1879759727,
			"version": 410,
			"versionNonce": 261928527,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": null,
			"updated": 1785622036900,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Feature            | Load Balancer                                 | API Gateway                                   |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Definition         | Distributes traffic across multiple servers   | Manages and routes client requests to APIs    |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Working            | Selects a healthy backend for each request    | Applies API rules before forwarding requests  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Primary purpose    | Improves availability and traffic distribution| Provides centralized API management           |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Routing basis      | Routes by IP, port, protocol, host, or path    | Routes by API path, method, header, or policy  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Authentication     | Usually relies on other services              | Commonly validates API keys, tokens, or OAuth  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Rate limiting      | Basic connection or request limits            | Supports client-level quotas and throttling   |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Protocol support   | Handles TCP, UDP, HTTP, and HTTPS              | Mainly handles HTTP, HTTPS, and WebSocket APIs |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Health checks      | Checks backends and avoids unhealthy servers  | May use service health information for routing|\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Data transformation| Usually forwards requests without changes    | Can transform request and response formats    |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Observability      | Tracks traffic, latency, and backend health   | Tracks API usage, errors, users, and quotas    |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Key difference     | Balances traffic between service instances    | Controls access and behavior at the API layer  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Advantages         | High availability, scalability, and failover  | Security, routing, throttling, and monitoring  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Disadvantages      | Limited API-specific management features      | Adds complexity, cost, and processing latency  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Best use cases     | Scaling web servers and service replicas      | Managing microservices and public APIs         |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Example            | NGINX distributing requests across web servers| Kong routing authenticated requests to services|\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+",
			"rawText": "+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Feature            | Load Balancer                                 | API Gateway                                   |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Definition         | Distributes traffic across multiple servers   | Manages and routes client requests to APIs    |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Working            | Selects a healthy backend for each request    | Applies API rules before forwarding requests  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Primary purpose    | Improves availability and traffic distribution| Provides centralized API management           |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Routing basis      | Routes by IP, port, protocol, host, or path    | Routes by API path, method, header, or policy  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Authentication     | Usually relies on other services              | Commonly validates API keys, tokens, or OAuth  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Rate limiting      | Basic connection or request limits            | Supports client-level quotas and throttling   |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Protocol support   | Handles TCP, UDP, HTTP, and HTTPS              | Mainly handles HTTP, HTTPS, and WebSocket APIs |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Health checks      | Checks backends and avoids unhealthy servers  | May use service health information for routing|\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Data transformation| Usually forwards requests without changes    | Can transform request and response formats    |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Observability      | Tracks traffic, latency, and backend health   | Tracks API usage, errors, users, and quotas    |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Key difference     | Balances traffic between service instances    | Controls access and behavior at the API layer  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Advantages         | High availability, scalability, and failover  | Security, routing, throttling, and monitoring  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Disadvantages      | Limited API-specific management features      | Adds complexity, cost, and processing latency  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Best use cases     | Scaling web servers and service replicas      | Managing microservices and public APIs         |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Example            | NGINX distributing requests across web servers| Kong routing authenticated requests to services|\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+",
			"fontSize": 36,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "center",
			"verticalAlign": "middle",
			"containerId": "XG4N8StMBTaJTv-5qyL_2",
			"originalText": "+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Feature            | Load Balancer                                 | API Gateway                                   |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Definition         | Distributes traffic across multiple servers   | Manages and routes client requests to APIs    |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Working            | Selects a healthy backend for each request    | Applies API rules before forwarding requests  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Primary purpose    | Improves availability and traffic distribution| Provides centralized API management           |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Routing basis      | Routes by IP, port, protocol, host, or path    | Routes by API path, method, header, or policy  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Authentication     | Usually relies on other services              | Commonly validates API keys, tokens, or OAuth  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Rate limiting      | Basic connection or request limits            | Supports client-level quotas and throttling   |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Protocol support   | Handles TCP, UDP, HTTP, and HTTPS              | Mainly handles HTTP, HTTPS, and WebSocket APIs |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Health checks      | Checks backends and avoids unhealthy servers  | May use service health information for routing|\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Data transformation| Usually forwards requests without changes    | Can transform request and response formats    |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Observability      | Tracks traffic, latency, and backend health   | Tracks API usage, errors, users, and quotas    |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Key difference     | Balances traffic between service instances    | Controls access and behavior at the API layer  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Advantages         | High availability, scalability, and failover  | Security, routing, throttling, and monitoring  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Disadvantages      | Limited API-specific management features      | Adds complexity, cost, and processing latency  |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Best use cases     | Scaling web servers and service replicas      | Managing microservices and public APIs         |\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| Example            | NGINX distributing requests across web servers| Kong routing authenticated requests to services|\n+--------------------+-----------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
